improve: We proceed to demonstrate the following theorem:\\\\
\textit{A quantized linear oscillator possesses a non-zero minimum energy level.}
\begin{proof}
To quantize the energy, we assume that its increments (either increase or decrease) occur in discrete, unitary packets. Thus in \q{in} we convert the continuous upper bound denoted by $a$ into a discrete upper bound represented by $n$, a non-negative integer. This integral represents the same values of the continuous upper bound integral, but only at equidistant points on a $n\times n$ square grid in the plane, where each square has a unit area. We can express the total energy $n^2/2$ in an alternative form that highlights the individual contributions of this representation by using the formula
\begin{align}
\sum_{j=1}^nj=\frac{n^2}{2}+\frac{n}{2}.
\end{align}
This expression overestimates the triangle area, because it include the complete areas of the unit squares intersected by the diagonal. To correct this, we rearrange the equation as follows  
\begin{align}
\sum_{j=1}^nj-\frac{n}{2}=\sum_{j=1}^n\left(j-\frac{1}{2}\right)=\frac{n^2}{2}.
\end{align}
It is now apparent that the total area under the diagonal can be correctly thought of as the sum of subareas consisting of columns, each contains a half-quare of area 1/2. Each column incrementally contributes $(j-1/2)$ to the energy within the triangle.  The expression $H_n=n^2/2$ represents the discrete dimensionless quantum Hamiltonian, which corresponds to the sum of the first $n$ possible energies arranged in increasing order. 
The relative increase in energy between two successive states can be determined by evaluating the integral 
\begin{align}
\int_n^{n+1} jdj=\left(n+\frac{1}{2}\right)\!,\;n=0,1,2,\dots
\end{align}
This integral provides the correct numerical progression to be used in Planck's formula. 
 When $n=0$, the expression reveals that the minimum energy of $1/2$ cannot be reached by an oscillator starting from zero energy, and vice versa, given the assumptions we made. Furthermore, an oscillator with zero energy cannot absorb a unit energy packet from the environment since doing so would necessitate occupying  the area of $1/2$ above the diagonal, which bounds the energy area within the triangle. We conclude that a zero-energy oscillator would not interact with the environment, rendering it irrelevant to the physical theory, and thus, it cannot exist.

